Let $x$ be Chris's current age. We can write a system of equations to represent the problem:

\begin{align*}
x+y+z&=12\\
y+4&=x\quad\Rightarrow\quad y=0
\end{align*} We want to find $x=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad y=0$, so we simply need to solve the equations \begin{align*}
x+z&=12\\
y&=0
\end{align*} From the first equation, we have that $z=12-1=x$. So, Chris is $\boxed{12}$ years old.
We note that since $y$ is a constant, the sum of their ages has no bearing on the problem and hence it doesn't matter what $x$ and $y$ are. We can just solve the second equation to find that $y=0$ and substitute into the first equation to find that $x=z=12$.
It's easy to solve this system using the matrices $\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix}1&12\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ to find that the inverse of the first equation is \[\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1/12&-1/12&0\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix}\] and the inverse of the second equation is \[\begin{pmatrix}1&12\\0&1\end{pmatrix}^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-12\\0&1\end{pmatrix}\] Now, we can substitute these inverses into the equations to find that \begin{align*}
x&=(1/12)(1/12)\cdot12=-1/12\cdot0+1\\
&=\boxed{1/12}
\end{align*}
