The smallest set of positive integers whose product is 84 is simply 1, 2, and 21. The sum of these integers is $1 + 2 + 21 = \boxed{24}$.
This is the minimal case possible, and all the possible sets of positive integers with a product of 84 also have a sum of 24.
Note that if you consider the case in which only 1 and 21 (not 1, 2), the sum of all such sets is still $\boxed{24}$.
For example, the sum of the set $\{1,2,21\}$ is $\boxed{24}$, whereas the sum of the set $\{2,21\}$ is $2+21=\boxed{23}$.
Note that this can be achieved without choosing 1 and 21.
Furthermore, the minimal case with a sum of 24 is $\boxed{1,2,21}$.
It is also possible to find $n$ and $m$ such that $mn=84$, but the larger of $n$ and $m$ will be greater than 21, which is the largest of those $n$ and $m$ in the minimal case.
Finally, note that there are $\lfloor\sqrt{84}\rfloor=9$ positive divisors of 84, namely 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 12, 14, and 21.  If we pick 1 and 21 randomly, then the least possible sum is 21.  If we then pick 2 in order, the sum goes up to 22, and so on, we can pick the first 9 numbers in order to get an sum of 24.
